WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:00.640
Hello there!

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.480
OpenAI’s CLIP and DALL-E have&nbsp;
been out for centuries now.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:16.272
What? Centuries? Ms. Coffee Bean, it’s been only 3&nbsp;
weeks now, but I think you all agree with us that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.272 --> 00:00:24.320
3 weeks in machine learning feel like a century!
Meanwhile Google published the Switch Transformer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:32.160
of 1 Trillion parameters, Transformers generate&nbsp;
music, Transformers generate 3D Dance motion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.880
Transformers detect 3D objects....&nbsp;
And the list goes on and on.

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:43.600
So let’s stay cool and avoid being overwhelmed.&nbsp;
In a previous video where we discussed OpenAI’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:50.480
Dall-E, we found out that it is great at&nbsp;
generating images from natural language.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:57.520
And it might look like creativity especially when&nbsp;
combining concepts like "armchair" and "avocado".&nbsp;

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:02.240
To make sure that DALL-E’s generations&nbsp;
are fitting the description well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.640
OpenAI employed a separate model called&nbsp;
CLIP to rank the image generations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:14.480
after their similarity to the text.
And if you do not know about DALL-E yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:19.360
check out our video about it linked in&nbsp;
the description below. It's worth it!&nbsp;

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:25.280
In this video, we will talk about this&nbsp;
helper model of DALL-E called CLIP.&nbsp;

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:32.880
But do not be deceived by us calling it a "helper"&nbsp;
model; it is more than impressive also on its own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:38.720
Make sure to stay until the end where we will show&nbsp;
what amazing things people did already with CLIP.&nbsp;

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:45.920
CLIP stands for Contrastive Language–Image&nbsp;
Pre-training and yes, the paper has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.360
a different title altogether. Anyway, based on&nbsp;
the paper, we will be answering the questions:&nbsp;

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.160
What can CLIP do?
What are the ingredients for its success?&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.560
What are the limitations of&nbsp;
CLIP? Because there are some.&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.080
And what can we, mortals do with CLIP&nbsp;
and what applications already exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:14.640
based on CLIP? Because in comparison to&nbsp;
DALL-E, CLIP was already shared by OpenAI.&nbsp;

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:19.280
CLIP is very good at telling&nbsp;
if an image and a short&nbsp;

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:25.440
piece of text belong together or not.
But why do we want to know if a given text&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:31.840
and image fit well together in the first place?&nbsp;
Well, because this task formulation is general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:37.920
enough to derivate many other tasks from it:
Like image recognition on ImageNet, for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.800
The task there is to classify images. And this is&nbsp;
what neural networks have been been doing so far:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.840
They take all available classes in the dataset&nbsp;
(here the dog races) and up to 1000 other classes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:59.040
and assign a probability for each class. The&nbsp;
highest scoring class is the classification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:05.840
prediction of the model, here "king Charles&nbsp;
spaniel". But with CLIP it gets even better:&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:11.440
Because of the way it was trained, CLIP is not&nbsp;
restricted to the classes from the dataset,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.440
but knows virtually all English words, being&nbsp;
able to formulates the ImageNet classes into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:23.520
prompts containing more language than just&nbsp;
the classes. And here we already see why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:28.400
CLIP has the potential to be so much&nbsp;
better than it’s competitors: Firstly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:35.600
it can also model the notion of “photo” or “image”&nbsp;
or “satellite”, extending it’s capabilities far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.600 --> 00:03:41.920
over a restricted set of classes, showing&nbsp;
great zero-shot performance on unseen datasets.&nbsp;

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:48.800
Secondly, CLIP is during training never restricted&nbsp;
to reduce an image to a single concept or word,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.800 --> 00:03:54.960
therefore it never loses or forgets other aspects&nbsp;
of the image that are not captured in the class,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:04:00.880
like the grass or wood on the background.
Impressively, but not perfectly, CLIP can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:06.960
solve tasks and datasets it has not explicitly&nbsp;
seen during training, like optical character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:14.400
recognition, geo-localization, texture detection&nbsp;
and others like facial emotion recognition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.920
and action recognition. It is impressive zero-shot&nbsp;
performance for a model trained only to predict a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.920
similarity between a text prompt and an image!
Then what are the ingredients to CLIPs success?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:31.760
Here we have to say that the&nbsp;
employed ideas are not really novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:37.200
there are a lot of ideas used in the CLIP&nbsp;
paper that where already used in research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:43.520
And OpenAI’s merit is definitely that it managed&nbsp;
to put everything together in a fine piece of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:50.720
engineering, which CLIP is (whisper: and DALL-E!).
Yes, the zero-shot capabilities of CLIP are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:58.000
impressive, but not unexpected; we have seen it&nbsp;
happen too with GPT-3 trained on huge amounts of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:06.960
data with high-variance: CLIP has been trained on&nbsp;
400 million images with paired text descriptions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:14.400
This is 100 times more data than ImageNet,&nbsp;
and it is very diversified data too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.680
because as it was all scraped from the internet.
So, the first ingredient for to the success&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:25.200
of CLIP is of course, the data. The more data&nbsp;
the better and the more diversified the data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:30.080
the more we can expect the model to be&nbsp;
able to perform afterwards in zero-shot.&nbsp;

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:37.440
Then on this data, CLIP was trained like this:&nbsp;
The authors take a batch of images and a batch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:43.760
of their corresponding textual descriptions.&nbsp;
The images are encoded, by either a ResNet or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:50.320
a Transformer and the text is encoded by a&nbsp;
transformer too. Then for each image in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:57.040
batch the encoder computes an image vector:&nbsp;
so the first image corresponds to the vector&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:04.560
I_1, the nth image to I_n and the same happens&nbsp;
for the textual description: the first textual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:11.440
sequence is encoded by one vector T_1, the nth&nbsp;
description by T_n. Then the magic happens:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.680
n square similarities are computed&nbsp;
between all these images and text vectors.&nbsp;

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:24.160
This means that the diagonal&nbsp;
elements should be maximized by CLIP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:34.240
as I1 fits with T1, I2 with T2 and so on.&nbsp;
In a contrastive fashion, the off-diagonal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.240 --> 00:06:39.120
elements should be minimized, since&nbsp;
we assume that is really unlikely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.640
that an image I1 fits with a random&nbsp;
other description other than its own.&nbsp;

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:50.640
And this the second ingredient for the success&nbsp;
of CLIP is the idea of predicting similarities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:55.280
between images and textual descriptions&nbsp;
and training in a contrastive fashion.&nbsp;

00:06:56.320 --> 00:07:04.080
But this idea is not new at all but have been&nbsp;
seeing this idea in Multimodal Transformers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:10.880
as a pretraining task, like for example in&nbsp;
LXMERT or ViLBERT, VisualBERT and so on.&nbsp;

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:15.840
So this is sadly some piece of related&nbsp;
work that we think the authors did not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:22.640
acknowledge enough and compare to... well…
The third element that the authors advertise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:30.080
CLIP with, is it’s computational efficiency:&nbsp;
Especially when these image and text encoders are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:37.120
transformers, we know that a lot of computations&nbsp;
can run in parallel compared to LSTMS for example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:43.360
But do not confound computational efficiency with&nbsp;
data efficiency: especially with transformers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:49.680
that do not have inductive biases that help during&nbsp;
learning from little data, they need more data to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:56.160
learn well. So they are not that data-efficient.
So the fourth ingredient to CLIP’s success comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.840
from the image and text representations&nbsp;
gained during training on the given data:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.960
CLIP now trained to predict high&nbsp;
similarity for fitting image-text pairs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:14.000
and low similarity for random ones is ready to be&nbsp;
applied on various tasks, like image recognition.&nbsp;

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:19.600
We remember, image recognition only&nbsp;
has one word to describe one image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:27.040
CLIP rather likes more words, like a description,
so the authors create a prompt, “a photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:33.840
of a” and then they insert all possible objects,&nbsp;
creating as many prompts as there are objects.&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.040
Then, they compute zero-shot the&nbsp;
similarity between a say, ImageNet image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:45.120
and these text prompts that differ only in the&nbsp;
mentioned object. Then the prompt and object with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:51.600
the highest similarity is chosen as prediction.
And the same CLIP model performs really well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:57.280
not only on the classical ImageNet image&nbsp;
recognition challenge, but on many other tasks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.840
with different kinds of images, or styles because&nbsp;
first: it has seen these image styles during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:10.080
training and secondly it has seen the words to&nbsp;
describe these kind of images and their details.&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:16.960
Ok, so CLIP can do so much, and it is time to&nbsp;
see what the limitations are of CLIP: Here we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:23.440
must refer to the "Limitations" section of the&nbsp;
CLIP paper, because it is the most entertaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.440 --> 00:09:30.560
limitations section we have ever read. We&nbsp;
include the passages we refer to and cite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:37.440
so you do not think that the used language is&nbsp;
really ours: no, they really, really formulate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:44.480
things like we show them here. Sot he authors make&nbsp;
clear that zero-shot CLIP is competitive with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:52.640
baseline of a supervised ResNet-50 model, trained&nbsp;
specifically on the training set of that dataset.&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:59.840
And this is impressive, because it means that CLIP&nbsp;
trained on OpenAIs dataset of 400 million images&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:07.280
absolves us a little of training specifically&nbsp;
on tasks and the datasets associated with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.920 --> 00:10:15.280
But here the authors warn us, that there is still&nbsp;
work needed to beat the supervised baselines. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:20.800
this work can have limitations if it&nbsp;
is done how the authors did it so far:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:28.000
they warn us that if we scale the model and&nbsp;
the data like they did so far, we would need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:34.480
a 1000 times more resources in compute,&nbsp;
which is infeasible with current hardware.&nbsp;

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:39.680
Then, the second limitation they tell us&nbsp;
about, is that: while CLIP is performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:47.760
amazingly on some zero-shot tasks, it&nbsp;
is not amazing on all tasks: We cite:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:53.920
"The performance of CLIP is poor on several&nbsp;
types of fine-graind classification such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:59.760
differentiating models of cars, species of flowers&nbsp;
and variants of aircraft." And CLIP has also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.760 --> 00:11:05.280
trouble with "abstract and systematic tasks such&nbsp;
as counting the number of objects in an image."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:13.760
CLIP has also trouble with tasks that are unlikely&nbsp;
in the pretraining dataset by saying that CLIP has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.760 --> 00:11:20.480
troubles generalizing to out-of-distribution&nbsp;
images. And they illustrate with an example of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:26.480
optical character recognition, where CLIP is&nbsp;
really good at recognizing digitally rendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.480 --> 00:11:32.800
text, but has troubles with MNIST, which&nbsp;
is a dataset containing handwritten digits.&nbsp;

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:39.520
And the reason the authors can identify is&nbsp;
that there are a lot of digitally rendered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:45.040
text images in CLIP's training data, but&nbsp;
not so much handwritten digits and CLIP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:49.920
kind of fails to generalize from digitally&nbsp;
rendered text to handwritten text.&nbsp;

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:57.040
And kind-of the next limitation they address&nbsp;
is that CLIP is not a caption generation model.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:03.120
It can only tell you how a given, an existing&nbsp;
image and an existing text fit together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.120 --> 00:12:09.840
but it cannot really compose new text.
And, also a very beautiful formulation:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:15.440
CLIP also, I cite: "CLIP does not address&nbsp;
the poor data efficiency of deep learning.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:21.360
Instead CLIP compensates by using a&nbsp;
source of supervision that can be scaled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:28.320
to hundreds of millions of training examples."
So CLIP didn't solve any of the deep learning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:34.080
problems, but it kind-of chose a&nbsp;
bigger hammer for solving the problem.&nbsp;

00:12:34.080 --> 00:12:39.200
Okay, but now that we know what the authors&nbsp;
found out that CLIP cannot do, let’s have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.200 --> 00:12:44.960
short look at what people have been able to do&nbsp;
with the CLIP model that was released by OpenAI:&nbsp;

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:55.040
And the first application we want to show you, is&nbsp;
this one by lab member 001 who was able to discern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.040 --> 00:13:02.080
between grainy, noisy photos and high-quality&nbsp;
photos. He could really nicely formulate in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.080 --> 00:13:09.200
natural language, because CLIP understands that.
So in a sense CLIP can be used as an image search&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:16.800
engine, as for example Vladimir did, who used CLIP&nbsp;
to retrieve car images, but it gets even better:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:21.760
He could retrieve even more detailed things,&nbsp;
like "car driving in the woods". So this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:26.400
the best thing about CLIP: You can describe&nbsp;
to the model very detailed what you want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:31.120
And you can retrieve pictures like this&nbsp;
one, where it is raining cats and dogs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:37.760
and also what I really liked is this example&nbsp;
of detecting chemical elements in pictures.&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:43.840
CLIP delivers us similarities or scores,&nbsp;
between an image and a text, so it can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:50.880
easily used as a discriminator in a GAN.&nbsp;
Here is a super-nice example of a Montreal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:56.080
image. Find the links to the tweets I have&nbsp;
shown you so far in the description below.&nbsp;

00:13:56.080 --> 00:14:01.120
And also in the description, please find the&nbsp;
link to this Google Colab released by OpenAI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.120 --> 00:14:06.240
where you get working code that you&nbsp;
can explore in your own appplication!&nbsp;

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:10.320
There are detailed instructions how&nbsp;
to download and initialize the model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:14.880
and then there is even an example&nbsp;
how to calculate similarities between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:22.240
images and pieces of text and also how to create&nbsp;
your own ImageNet-like classifier with CLIP!

00:14:22.240 --> 00:14:27.280
So you can play with CLIP on Colab yourself,&nbsp;
do not forget to find all the links mentioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:32.880
in the description below. Let us know what cool&nbsp;
things you have created with CLIP in the comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:47.418
and see you next time!
